content:
maria: Heads-up: SDK v0.9.2 clients hitting sporadic "Unexpected end of JSON input" when consuming SSE from staging. Looks like server is splitting mid-UTF8/codepoint sometimes. Also seeing frontend CPU spikes when we try to throttle reads. anyone seen this windowing/backpressure combo?
kevin: Which runtime/browser? and are you using the streaming parser or just JSON.parse on chunks?
maria: repro in Chrome and Node. We were doing simple chunk-append + try JSON.parse on 
buffered.join('') when we see a full "}" — naive and obviously brittle.
alex: yeah that approach breaks with split multibyte sequences. use TextDecoder with streaming decodes or a streaming JSON parser that handles partial tokens.
sam: to add: in node streams you can control highWaterMark/resume/pause to avoid mem spikes. in browser fetch ReadableStream you can also backpressure by not calling reader.read() until you're ready. but some polyfills/bundlers change behavior.
maria: failing repro attached: small server writes ~40-120ms frames, sometimes writes 7 bytes then 200 bytes. the client fails ~10% of runs. curious about detection heuristics for safe decode boundary.
leo: quick tip: detect invalid UTF-8 tail by attempting a decode with TextDecoder and catching the replacement char \uFFFD. but better is TextDecoder.decode(chunk, {stream: true}) and accumulate the output string. don't feed partial bytes into JSON.parse.
natalie: example (browser):
```js
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let text = '';
const reader = (await fetch(url)).body.getReader();
while(true){
  const {value, done} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  text += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
  // split on newline/ndjson boundary
  const pieces = text.split('\n');
  text = pieces.pop(); // last may be partial
  for(const line of pieces){
    if(line.trim()) handleJSON(JSON.parse(line));
  }
}
if(text) handleJSON(JSON.parse(text));
```
kevin: that looks good. also consider a tiny framing byte like `\n` or length-prefix if you control server. ensures no ambiguity.
maria: server is remote infra so we prefer not to add framing. any suggestions if we must handle arbitrary splits?
sam: add a quick UTF-8 boundary check: if the last byte of the buffer is an incomplete multibyte header, keep it for next chunk. psuedocode: check last byte & 0b11100000 etc to find sequence length and ensure enough continuation bytes present.
leo: or use existing library e.g., clarinet / oboe / jsonparse for streaming-js. they do incremental tokenization and are robust to splits. less likely to regresses than ad-hoc heuristics.
maria: ok will try TextDecoder+ndjson split first, then swap in clarinet if flakes remain. one more: what about the high CPU when throttling reads?
natalie: likely because your throttle loop still decodes/allocates large arrays but isn't consuming the stream, causing GC churn. solution: apply backpressure early: stop calling reader.read() until queue length below threshold. in node set socket.setNoDelay true? and tune highWaterMark.
kevin: also check if a ServiceWorker or proxy is coalescing TCP writes into bigger frames — that can change timing. capture raw bytes with a small server that base64 dumps frames so you can compare.
maria: useful. i'll do tests: 1) TextDecoder streaming + ndjson split, 2) clarinet, 3) node highWaterMark tweaks. will post results. thanks all :thumbsup:
ci-bot: deploy-preview/streaming-repro updated: https://ci.company/?pr=4012 :rocket:
maria: quick follow-up: if anyone has sample utf8-tail-check snippet i'd take it.
sam: paste from notes: 
```js
function trailingUtf8Bytes(buf){
  // buf is Uint8Array
  for(let i=1;i<=4 && i<=buf.length;i++){
    const b = buf[buf.length-i];
    if((b & 0b10000000) === 0) return 0; // single byte
    if((b & 0b11000000) === 0b11000000){
      const seqLen = (b & 0b11100000) === 0b11000000 ? 2
                    : (b & 0b11110000) === 0b11100000 ? 3 : 4;
      return Math.min(i-1, seqLen-1); // how many continuation bytes we have
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
```
leo: neat. tl;dr: prefer streaming decode libs, avoid naive chunk-JSON.parse, throttle by stopping reads, and test with base64 raw dump.
maria: will try all and report. thanks!
